<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.4.3: Adding and Subtracting Systems of Equations and Using Their Graphs</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00418</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.4.3: Adding and Subtracting Systems of Equations and Using Their Graphs</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7d692c1b-4deb-4797-82a5-9d725e887157</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (15 minutes)</title>
<para>Earlier, students saw that adding or subtracting the equations in a system creates a third equation that can help us solve the system. In this activity, students reconnect the idea of the solution to a system to the intersection of the graphs of the equations. They graph each original pair of equations and the equation that results from adding or subtracting them. They then observe that the graph of the third equation intersects the other two graphs at the identical point: the intersection of the first two.</para>
<para>At this point, students simply get a graphical confirmation that adding or subtracting equations can help them find the solution to a system. They are not yet expected to be able to articulate why this is the case. That understanding will be developed over a few upcoming lessons.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Remind students that earlier they added or subtracted pairs of equations to form new equations. Explain that they will now graph each pair of equations in the systems given earlier, as well as the third equation that came from adding or subtracting those equations. Then they will make some observations about them.</para>
<para>Arrange students into groups of 3 and provide access to graphing technology. Assign one system for each group member to graph. Ask students to discuss their observations after graphing.</para>
<para>If possible, make available graphing technology that allows users to enter linear equations in standard form, such as Desmos. Otherwise, give students time to rearrange the equations into a form that can be used with the technology and to check their equivalent equations. If time is limited, provide these equivalent equations:</para>
<para>System A</para>
<para>\( \begin {cases}\begin {align}4x+3y = 10\\-4x + 5y = 6 \end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>
<para>System B</para>
<para>\( \begin {cases}\begin {align}y &amp;= -2x+4\\ y&amp;=\hspace{3mm}x-11 \end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>
<para>System C</para>
<para>\( \begin {cases}\begin {align}y &amp;= -\frac{8}{11}x+\frac{37}{11}\\ y&amp;=\hspace{3.7mm}-8x + 7 \end{align} \end{cases} \)</para>

<note class="extra-support">
  <title>Support for Students with Disabilities</title><para>Action and Expression: Internalize Executive Functions </para>
  <para>To support development of organizational skills, check in with students within the first 2 – 3 minutes of work time. Look for students who are able to find the sum or difference of the equations with ease. If students struggle, direct students to use parentheses around the entirety of the equation being added or subtracted to enhance clarity and reduce errors.</para>
      <para><emphasis effect="italics">Supports accessibility for: Memory; Organization</emphasis></para></note>


<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Below are three systems of equations you saw earlier.</para>
<para>Use System A to answer questions 1–6.</para>
<para>System A</para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{c}4x+3y=10\;\\-4x+5y=6\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equation that represents this scenario. Students were provided access to Desmos. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/71e8c2e76c0c148e09a4857b0ddf62c81ee77134"/></media></figure><list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Identify the \(x\) coordinate of the solution. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 1</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> Identify the \(y\) coordinate of the solution. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 2</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> Find the sum or difference of the two original equations that would enable the system to be solved. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
<list>
  <item> Sample Answer 1: \(8y=16 \)</item>
  <item> Sample Answer 2: \(y=2 \)</item>
  <item> Sample Answer 3: \(8x−2y=4 \)</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> Graph the third equation on the same coordinate plane. Students were provided access to Desmos. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of a linear system."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/8a942e0e69c4e77278807606033a9fd90613bb3b"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> Make an observation about the graph. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
<para>All three lines intersect at \((1, 2)\). If students graph \(8y=16\), the graph of the new equation is a horizontal line. If students graph \(8x−2y=4\), the graph of the new equation is a new line between the two original lines.</para>

<para>Use System B to answer questions 7–12.</para>
<para>System B</para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{c}2x+y=4\\x-y=11\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item> Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equation that represents this scenario. Students were provided access to Desmos. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of a linear system."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/07785e38d47bfd086d792491687c0f1381c09c29"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item> Identify the \(x\) coordinate of the solution. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 5</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item> Identify the \(y\) coordinate of the solution. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -6</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item> Find the sum or difference of the two original equations that would enable the system to be solved. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
<list>
  <item> Sample Answer 1: \(3x=15 \)</item>
  <item> Sample Answer 2: \(x=5 \)</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated">
  <item> Graph the third equation on the same coordinate plane. Students were provided access to Desmos. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of a linear system."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4791d4d27317a14a058f9373881c4584c9e4669a"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="12" type="enumerated">
  <item> Make an observation about the graph. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. All three lines intersect at \((5, -6)\). If students graph \(3x=15\), the graph of the new equation is a vertical line.</para>

<para>Use System C to answer questions 13–18.</para>
<para>System C</para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{c}8x+11y=37\\8x+y=7\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="13" type="enumerated">
  <item> Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the equation that represents this scenario. Students were provided access to Desmos. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of a linear system."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/44ebd2bb4d629f315f8a352bf375bb4d8ff9b12e"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="14" type="enumerated">
  <item> Identify the \(x\) coordinate of the solution. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(\frac12\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="15" type="enumerated">
  <item> Identify the \(y\) coordinate of the solution. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 3</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="16" type="enumerated">
  <item> Find the sum or difference of the two original equations that would enable the system to be solved. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
<list>
  <item> Sample Answer 1: \(10y=30 \)</item>
  <item> Sample Answer 2: \(−10y=−30 \)</item>
  <item> Sample Answer 3: \(y=3 \)</item>
  <item> Sample Answer 4: (If the equations are added) \(16x+12y=44 \)</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="17" type="enumerated">
  <item> Graph the third equation on the same coordinate plane. Students were provided access to Desmos. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para><figure><media alt="Graph of a linear system.Graph of a linear system."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/680c69a559ee7e9bd444ef9bd3adf1cd1e955390"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="18" type="enumerated">
  <item> Make an observation about the graph. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. All three lines intersect at \((\frac12,3)\). If students graph \(10y=30\), the graph of the new equation is a horizontal line.</para>
<title>Student Facing Extension</title>
<title>Are you ready for more?</title>
<para>Mai wonders what would happen if we multiply equations. That is, we multiply the expressions on the left side of the two equations and set them equal to the expressions on the right side of the two equations.</para>
<para>Use System B to answer questions 1–4.</para>
<para>System B</para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{c}2x+y=4\\x-y=11\end{array}\right.\)</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Write out an equation that you would get if you multiply the two equations in this manner. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Your answer may vary, but here is a sample. \((2x+y)(x−y)=44\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Does your original solution still work in this new equation? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> Use graphing technology to graph this new equation on the same coordinate plane. Students were provided access to Desmos. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para><figure><media alt="A system of equations."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/efaaa21ce115d24d626d1fbe16deb365977e88c4"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> Explain if the approach of graphing the new equation is or is not particularly helpful. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> This approach is not helpful because the new equation is not a line and therefore not so easy to work with.</para>
<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>When solving system B, some students may not notice that the \( y \)-variable in one equation has a positive coefficient and the other has a negative coefficient. Consequently, they may decide to subtract the second equation from the first rather than to add the two equations. They may struggle to figure out why the solution pair they find doesn’t match what is on the graph. Suggest that they express the second equation in terms of addition, \( x+ (-y) = 11 \), and try eliminating one variable again.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Display the graphs that students generated for all to see and ask students to share their observations. Highlight that the graph of the new equation intersects the graphs of the equations in the original system at the same point.</para>
<para>Time permitting, ask students to subtract the equations they previously added (or add the equations they previously subtracted) and then to graph the resulting equation on the same coordinate plane. Ask them to comment on the graphs. Students are likely to see that the graphs of the new equations are no longer horizontal or vertical lines, but they still intersect at the same point as the original graphs.</para>
<para>Invite students to make some conjectures as to why the graph of the new equation intersects the other two graphs at the same point. Without confirming or correcting their conjectures, tell students that they will investigate this question in the coming activities.</para>
<title>2.4.3: Self Check</title>
<para><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para>QUESTION:</para>
<para>Solve the system of equations using elimination. </para>
<para>Check the solution by graphing. </para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;&amp;\left\{\begin{array}{l}2x\;–\;4y\;=\;–26\\–3x\;+\;4y\;=\;29\end{array}\right.\end{array}\)</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Answers</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Feedback</emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry> \((-8, 2.5)\) </entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Is the ordered pair a solution for both equations? The answer is \((-3, 5)\). </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> \((-3, 5)\) </entry>
      <entry> That’s correct! Check yourself: The ordered pair, \((-3, 5)\), is a solution to both equations in the system. </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> \((0, 6.25)\) </entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Is the ordered pair a solution for both equations? The answer is \((-3, 5)\). </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> \((-13, 0)\) </entry>
      <entry> Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Is the ordered pair a solution for both equations? The answer is \((-3, 5)\). </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>2.4.3: Additional Resources</title>

<para><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Adding Systems of Equations and Using Their Graphs</title>
<para>Look at the system of equations shown here:</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}&amp;&amp;\left\{\begin{array}{l}–3x\;+\;4y\;=\;7\\3x\;+\;2y\;=\;17\end{array}\right.\end{array}\)</para>
<para>Let’s graph each equation on the coordinate plane. Identify the point of intersection.</para>
<figure><media alt="GRAPH OF TWO LINES INTERSECTING AT THE POINT (3, 4)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/5f191fe3bedabe49601739750eeed7490efb4923"/></media></figure><emphasis/>
<para>The lines intersect at \((3, 4)\).</para>
<para>Add the two equations together. Simplify the resulting equation.</para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{r}-3x+4y=7\\3x+2y=17\\\hline6y=24\\y=4\end{array}\) </para>
<para>Graph the resulting equation on the same coordinate plane.</para>
  <figure><media alt="GRAPH OF THREE LINES INTERSECTING AT THE POINT (3,4)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/0dce7e1312aff88580115970deb60e42d6fcfc3a"/></media></figure><emphasis/>

<para>All three lines intersect at \((3, 4)\). </para>
<para>You verified that by adding the two equations in the system, you created a third equation that is also true.</para>
<para>The solution to the system is \(x = 3\), \(y = 4\).</para>
<title>Try It: Adding Systems of Equations and Using Their Graphs</title>
<para>Add the equations to find the resulting equation. Then graph all three equations to find the ordered pair where they intersect.</para>
<para>Use this system of equations to answer questions 1–4.</para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{c}-2x-3y=7\\2x+y=3\end{array}\right.\)</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Add the equations to find the resulting equation. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>Compare your answer.</para>
<para> \(\underline{\;\;\;\;\;-2x-3y=7\\+\;\;\;\;\;\;\;2x+y=3}\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;-2y\;=10\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;y\;=-5\\\) </para>

<para> \(9\begin{array}{rcl}2x+\;y&amp;=&amp;3\;\\2x+\;(-5)&amp;=&amp;3\\2x&amp;=&amp;8\\x&amp;=&amp;4\\&amp;&amp;\end{array}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Graph this new equation on the same coordinate plane. Students were provided access to Desmos. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para><figure><media alt="GRAPH OF THREE LINES THAT INTERSECT AT THE POINT (4,-5)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/873c5688c0f868097e46c8b904cacff1ccbe9ab7"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> Identify the \(x\) coordinate of the solution. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 4</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> Identify the \(y\) coordinate of the solution. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -5</para>
</section>
